{"version":3,"kind":"Notebook","sha256":"7d1e6024969b259bbe706736f4da63d6dd61f2ff536a13611e2b2a6e43ce5057","slug":"pd-sns","location":"/04_all/04_03-pd_sns.ipynb","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Part 3. Other complementary libraries","content_includes_title":false,"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"pyqm","language":"python"},"authors":[{"id":"Silvia M. Gimenez-Santamarina","name":"Silvia M. Gimenez-Santamarina"}],"github":"https://github.com/silsgs/pyqm-textbook_26-27","keywords":["jupyter-book"],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"ipynb","filename":"04_03-pd_sns.ipynb","url":"/pyqm-textbook_26-27/build/04_03-pd_sns-75dfda7867e1d17bea6a767dbf119e4a.ipynb"}]},"widgets":{},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two other complementary libraries that we have already mentioned, which are also extensively used in scientific computing with Python: Pandas and Seaborn.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"GazPUjF8FX"}],"key":"EEJZCJYVXM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pandas is a good library for data manipulation and analysis, providing a specific type of data structures called DataFrames. DataFrames allow you to store and manipulate tabular data in a flexible and efficient way, allowing for strings and numerical data all together. It offers a wide range of functionalities for data manipulation, including filtering, grouping, and aggregation. And it also provides Series, which are one-dimensional labeled arrays that make it easy to work with structured data.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"sfs1G5W5p4"}],"key":"qEqeAKlbtl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Seaborn is a statistical data visualization library built on top of Matplotlib. It provides high-quality and sophisticated designs for drawing plots, subplots, and informative statistical graphics.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"x63caDy8Wx"}],"key":"nEVmsUbzzX"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1 Pandas","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"gH0efWMkyx"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-pandas","label":"3.1 Pandas","html_id":"id-3-1-pandas","implicit":true,"key":"LxpTEE10To"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"First of all we will need to install Pandas library if we haven’t done so already. You can install it using the following command:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"JyESozTIBv"}],"key":"HnNaEyK1Nh"},{"type":"code","lang":"bash","value":"conda install pandas","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"jvtWOHPfAW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then, once we have installed Pandas, we can import it into our Python environment using the following command:","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"DOMqGHf2f4"}],"key":"c4uFBdqNlk"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"import pandas as pd","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"dWJlUa39ND"}],"key":"cKBFai0TFc"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.1 DataFrames","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"QpebD451se"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-1-dataframes","label":"3.1.1 DataFrames","html_id":"id-3-1-1-dataframes","implicit":true,"key":"SkHz7NpzqY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"DataFrames are the primary data structure in Pandas. They are two-dimensional, size-mutable, and potentially heterogeneous tabular data structures with labeled axes (rows and columns). They are very powerful as they allow for easy store and access of data, with column-wise and row-wise operations. However, for numerical computations, it is often more efficient to work with NumPy arrays directly. Here is an example of creating a simple DataFrame:","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"sRiX9X9S7v"}],"key":"av1H3B059I"}],"key":"tEyUyO8yAB"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = {\n    'Name': ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie'],\n    'Age': [25, 30, 35],\n    'City': ['New York', 'Los Angeles', 'Chicago']\n}\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\nprint(df)\n","key":"H5yqJM6yjR"},{"type":"outputs","id":"IOlXg739eojlD9vJyoQM5","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"      Name  Age         City\n0    Alice   25     New York\n1      Bob   30  Los Angeles\n2  Charlie   35      Chicago\n"},"children":[],"key":"NDyTNpRx6t"}],"key":"k1bI9vbC1i"}],"key":"vw4r4pwHP8"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.2 Series","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"eL02mPsxkx"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-2-series","label":"3.1.2 Series","html_id":"id-3-1-2-series","implicit":true,"key":"OX5lgaImKi"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Series is a one-dimensional array-like object that can hold any data type. It is similar to a column in a DataFrame. Each element in a Series has an associated label, called an index. Here is an example of creating a simple Series:","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"KM3QweInOv"}],"key":"j1vAycRrvY"}],"key":"kNQsMCoWbm"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nseries = pd.Series(data)\nprint(series)\n","key":"oM8BMPmpZL"},{"type":"outputs","id":"qDwJQvqKEdm1QXGL1fEMX","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"0    10\n1    20\n2    30\n3    40\n4    50\ndtype: int64\n"},"children":[],"key":"BEk9p1Iv1o"}],"key":"hNxiAsZWhq"}],"key":"moAcd3Q6k4"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most important feature of a Series is its index, which allows for fast lookups and alignment of data. You can specify the index explicitly or let pandas create a default one. Here is an example with a custom index:","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"p1iclu30S3"}],"key":"ErnhDZ6aru"}],"key":"XPqS11NBYP"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nindex = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']\nseries = pd.Series(data, index=index)\nprint(series)","key":"i0rmIK5zLl"},{"type":"outputs","id":"Y0LfLZLqSJv9zAQ5JzalF","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"a    10\nb    20\nc    30\nd    40\ne    50\ndtype: int64\n"},"children":[],"key":"dwypT6ELif"}],"key":"z5s4zneVb4"}],"key":"Rl6WiuEqJa"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.3 Indexing and Slicing","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"GxRLIh05DU"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-3-indexing-and-slicing","label":"3.1.3 Indexing and Slicing","html_id":"id-3-1-3-indexing-and-slicing","implicit":true,"key":"NOteBuSh3H"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Indexing and slicing in a Series works similarly to lists and arrays in Python. You can use labels or integer positions to access elements. Here are some examples:","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"XRaJXwwwXV"}],"key":"xeJf3fNNXC"}],"key":"vyyb1gerzA"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nindex = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']\nseries = pd.Series(data, index=index)\n\n# Accessing elements by label\nprint(series['a'])  # Output: 10\n\n# Accessing elements by integer position\nprint(series[0])    # Output: 10\n\n# Slicing by label\nprint(series['a':'c'])  # Output: a    10\n                         #         b    20\n                         #         c    30\n\n# Slicing by integer position\nprint(series[0:3])      # Output: a    10\n                         #         b    20\n                         #         c    30","key":"BYccx2YaEX"},{"type":"outputs","id":"81a6DzZZ0_jqBTou3JFnr","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"10\n10\na    10\nb    20\nc    30\ndtype: int64\na    10\nb    20\nc    30\ndtype: int64\n"},"children":[],"key":"DygtdEpGSC"},{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":"C:\\Users\\silvi\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\ipykernel_30240\\3782460028.py:11: FutureWarning: Series.__getitem__ treating keys as positions is deprecated. In a future version, integer keys will always be treated as labels (consistent with DataFrame behavior). To access a value by position, use `ser.iloc[pos]`\n  print(series[0])    # Output: 10\n"},"children":[],"key":"BxCQcFu6dK"}],"key":"UpbD88HHLG"}],"key":"suPyZ0L7hT"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.4 Data Cleaning","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"gUo5mJ2PEy"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-4-data-cleaning","label":"3.1.4 Data Cleaning","html_id":"id-3-1-4-data-cleaning","implicit":true,"key":"mfcfZOkjuw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pandas provides several functions for cleaning and handling missing data in a Series. Common tasks include detecting missing values, filling them with a specific value, or dropping them altogether. Here are some examples:","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"fug6ZmHwzC"}],"key":"MyEIdTyH2a"}],"key":"kjvuMdybG6"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = [10, None, 30, None, 50]\nindex = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']\nseries = pd.Series(data, index=index)\n\n# Detecting missing values\nprint(series.isna())  # Output: a    False\n                      #         b     True\n                      #         c    False\n                      #         d     True\n                      #         e    False\n\n# Filling missing values\nseries_filled = series.fillna(0)\nprint(series_filled)  # Output: a    10\n                      #         b     0\n                      #         c    30\n                      #         d     0\n                      #         e    50\n\n# Dropping missing values\nseries_dropped = series.dropna()\nprint(series_dropped)  # Output: a    10\n                       #         c    30\n                       #         e    50","key":"Op0Ynd3VKD"},{"type":"outputs","id":"ooFwKOig3q7WlHKLkSZht","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"a    False\nb     True\nc    False\nd     True\ne    False\ndtype: bool\na    10.0\nb     0.0\nc    30.0\nd     0.0\ne    50.0\ndtype: float64\na    10.0\nc    30.0\ne    50.0\ndtype: float64\n"},"children":[],"key":"FuI9xx5oUX"}],"key":"xkDIrmwElr"}],"key":"yQp3VXBmSz"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.5 Merging and Joining","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"K4HNjlofrp"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-5-merging-and-joining","label":"3.1.5 Merging and Joining","html_id":"id-3-1-5-merging-and-joining","implicit":true,"key":"nuWSkuKh4j"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Merging and joining Series in pandas allows you to combine data from multiple Series objects based on their indices. Common operations include concatenation, merging, and joining. Here are some examples:","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"pUNcJalKwx"}],"key":"DlAWkQxhq7"}],"key":"zQCTSZdFsJ"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"\nimport pandas as pd\n\nseries1 = pd.Series([1, 2, 3], index=['a', 'b', 'c'])\nseries2 = pd.Series([4, 5, 6], index=['b', 'c', 'd'])\n\n# Concatenation\nconcatenated = pd.concat([series1, series2])\nprint(concatenated)\n\n# Merging (aligning by index)\nmerged = series1.add(series2, fill_value=0)\nprint(merged)","key":"Q6FJpb4qIP"},{"type":"outputs","id":"v8KIYDXQf1v8JWJ3vJB9U","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"a    1\nb    2\nc    3\nb    4\nc    5\nd    6\ndtype: int64\na    1.0\nb    6.0\nc    8.0\nd    6.0\ndtype: float64\n"},"children":[],"key":"zfMkix3pH3"}],"key":"WMIsyYOKzZ"}],"key":"QEDFs3EuOo"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.6 Grouping and Aggregation","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"NTeRTmnL0i"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-6-grouping-and-aggregation","label":"3.1.6 Grouping and Aggregation","html_id":"id-3-1-6-grouping-and-aggregation","implicit":true,"key":"g3sanLm53I"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping and aggregation in pandas allows you to group data based on certain criteria and then apply aggregate functions to summarize the data. Here is an example:","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"nxPcnQDm7b"}],"key":"Zg8Xt20k8j"}],"key":"qaNO4yAcSY"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"import pandas as pd\n\ndata = pd.Series([10, 20, 30, 40, 50], index=['a', 'b', 'a', 'b', 'a'])\n\n# Grouping by index and calculating the sum\ngrouped = data.groupby(data.index).sum()\nprint(grouped)  # Output: a    89\n                #         b    59\n","key":"iOPxU2lIRw"},{"type":"outputs","id":"ec3oQhtmqG-WYN2Ea9khN","children":[],"key":"q5tVdH2cUo"}],"key":"d0bxkdDMR8"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1.7 Time Series Analysis","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"tj8enzqzeL"}],"identifier":"id-3-1-7-time-series-analysis","label":"3.1.7 Time Series Analysis","html_id":"id-3-1-7-time-series-analysis","implicit":true,"key":"mOC8gSwtgQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time series analysis in pandas allows you to work with time-indexed data, perform resampling, and apply various time-based operations. Here is an example:","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"ksTcskoCkZ"}],"key":"oxzpHoAjUt"}],"key":"VKkqTXDoOP"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Creating a time series\ndates = pd.date_range('20230101', periods=6)\nts = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], index=dates)\n\n# Resampling the time series to a different frequency (e.g., daily to bi-daily)\nresampled = ts.resample('2D').sum()\nprint(resampled)","key":"rmiOoSOMXV"},{"type":"outputs","id":"hbpGLTBKH9xoKrENR_wNK","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"2023-01-01     3\n2023-01-03     7\n2023-01-05    11\nFreq: 2D, dtype: int64\n"},"children":[],"key":"nSIMJVlddB"}],"key":"wfMZKusY5j"}],"key":"AXEuwWDIOY"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.2 Seaborn","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"XIuIJor5No"}],"identifier":"id-3-2-seaborn","label":"3.2 Seaborn","html_id":"id-3-2-seaborn","implicit":true,"key":"PYTiRCM0uL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Seaborn is a really good complementary library to numpy and pandas for creating attractive and informative statistical graphics. It provides a high-level interface for drawing various types of plots with ease.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"YrO6TxjWZM"}],"key":"oXUue1EQX6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As always, first we will need to install and import the Seaborn library.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"h7h7YXj11S"}],"key":"zrZ5Mx4ruY"},{"type":"code","lang":"bash","value":"conda install seaborn","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"GELJD2FkOU"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"import seaborn as sns","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"nN84fklwfi"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here you can find a full gallery of Seaborn plots: ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"cyycOusCpe"},{"type":"link","url":"https://seaborn.pydata.org/examples/index.html","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"https://​seaborn​.pydata​.org​/examples​/index​.html","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"GeWE2jwCIL"}],"urlSource":"https://seaborn.pydata.org/examples/index.html","key":"lxuQWXz7gl"}],"key":"x3S8DZYDmm"}],"key":"WcoUn4nSOk"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.2.1 Differences and similarities with Matplotlib","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"tKAeON240Q"}],"identifier":"id-3-2-1-differences-and-similarities-with-matplotlib","label":"3.2.1 Differences and similarities with Matplotlib","html_id":"id-3-2-1-differences-and-similarities-with-matplotlib","implicit":true,"key":"c6MRkQ8k7q"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The main difference between Matplotlib and Seaborn is that Matplotlib is a low-level plotting library that provides fine-grained control over plot elements, while Seaborn is a high-level interface built on top of Matplotlib that simplifies the creation of complex statistical plots with less code. Seaborn also comes with built-in themes and color palettes to make plots more visually appealing by default.\nWithin the Seaborn library, many functions are designed to work directly with Pandas DataFrames, making it easier to visualize data without extensive preprocessing.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"BIKlARNEpB"}],"key":"BJeQ67JDBQ"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.2.2 Statistical Plots","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"aLcKAMgab9"}],"identifier":"id-3-2-2-statistical-plots","label":"3.2.2 Statistical Plots","html_id":"id-3-2-2-statistical-plots","implicit":true,"key":"hcMSRb4kWD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can create various types of statistical plots using Seaborn, such as histograms, KDE plots, box plots, and violin plots. These plots help in understanding the distribution, relationships, and patterns within your data.\nFor example, to create a histogram of a dataset ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"wmkirSHBuM"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data","positi